<section n="1" image-layout="blank" id="480a10bc3k7wx6r">



<div class="body">
<h1 align="center">2026 Market Update</h1>
<p align="center">Redpoint Ventures — Spring 2026</p>
<smart-layout variant="solidJoinedBoxes">
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>AI CapEx</h4>
    <p>Tracking toward ~3.3% of GDP by 2030 — the largest tech investment wave in US history</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>Hyperscaler Spend</h4>
    <p>$309B in 2024 → $779B by 2027E at a 36% CAGR</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>Agent TAM</h4>
    <p>Autonomous agents unlock $6.1T+ in addressable market across software and payroll</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>Private Valuations</h4>
    <p>Seed median hit ~$60M; rounds closing at multi-year speed; 2026–27 is the historical sweet spot</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
</smart-layout></div></section>

<section n="2" image-layout="blank" id="ide3u1hvu13rv29">



    
  

<div class="body">
<h1>We're Living Through a Genuine Technological Revolution</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/ZE9UqtevDnVPzMUFucU2W.png" meta="null" resize="null" source="image.custom" align="center">
<smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine">
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>The Largest Investment Wave in US History</h4>
        <p>AI infrastructure capex is projected to reach <b>~3.3% of GDP by 2030</b> on the bull case — rivaling the railroad and telecom buildouts. Hyperscaler CapEx is surging from <b>$309B in 2024 to $779B by 2027E</b>, a 36% CAGR. Meta leads at 54% CAGR, Google at 49%, Microsoft at 33%.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>This Is Not Dotcom 2.0</h4>
        <p>OpenAI and Anthropic are each generating <b>$20B+ ARR</b>. Over 90% of data center capacity is pre-committed. Nvidia EPS stands at ~$4.06 — vs. Cisco's ~$0.37 at the 2000 peak. The fundamentals are categorically different from the bubble era.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Adoption at Unprecedented Speed</h4>
        <p>ChatGPT reached <b>1B+ monthly active users</b> roughly 4 years post-launch. The internet had only 70M users 4 years post-Netscape. This adoption curve has no historical precedent in consumer or enterprise technology.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
    </smart-layout>
<smart-layout variant="stats">
  <smart-layout-item number="$309B">
    <h4>2024A CapEx</h4>
    <p>Hyperscaler baseline</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item number="$779B">
    <h4>2027E CapEx</h4>
    <p>36% CAGR overall</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item number="54%">
    <h4>Meta CAGR</h4>
    <p>Fastest-growing hyperscaler</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item number="1B+">
    <h4>MAU ~4 Yrs</h4>
    <p>vs. 70M internet users post-Netscape</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
</smart-layout></div></section>

<section n="3" image-layout="blank" id="ohv32ybykdetpgb">


<div class="body">
<h1>Awe-Inspiring Products Are Shipping Weekly</h1>
<columns col-widths="[55, 45]">
  <div>
    <smart-layout variant="timeline" orientation="vertical">
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Jan '25 — DeepSeek R1</h4>
        <p>Crashed Nvidia stock ~17% in a single session. Proved frontier-quality reasoning at a fraction of incumbent cost — redefining the efficiency frontier for model development.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Apr '25 — Vibe Coding Goes Mainstream</h4>
        <p>Lovable, Replit, and Cursor enable non-technical users to ship full applications. Build cost collapsed from <b>$500K / 6 months (2015)</b> to <b>$50 / 6 hours (2026)</b> — a 10,000x efficiency gain.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>May '25 – Jul '25 — Science Breakthroughs</h4>
        <p>AlphaFold 3 designs novel protein binders for new drug candidates. OpenAI and Google achieve IMO Gold — surpassing the world's top mathematicians on international olympiad problems.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Feb '26 — Agents in the Physical World</h4>
        <p>Waymo operating in 10 US cities with 200M fully autonomous miles driven. Gemini 3.1 and Claude 4.6 deliver compounding reasoning and agentic capability breakthroughs.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
    </smart-layout>
  </div>
  <div>
    <img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/N6cyUnm9MCYPZGB6C5ndH.png" meta="null" resize="null" source="image.custom">
    <aside variant="success">Software developer demand is <b>rising, not falling</b>. Indeed job postings for software engineers recovered to <b>97% of Jan 2024 baseline</b> by Jan 2026 — outpacing total job postings at 85%. AI is expanding the developer economy.</aside>
  </div>
</columns></div></section>

<section n="4" image-layout="blank" id="ir87rsrhp07t0v5">


<div class="body">
<h1>Agents Are Still Early — But the TAM Unlocks Are Enormous</h1>
<columns col-widths="[50, 50]">
  <div>
    <p>We are largely at the <b>Task Agent</b> stage: AI executes discrete tasks end-to-end with human oversight, with independent runtimes measured in minutes. Each maturity level that follows unlocks a dramatically larger addressable market.</p>
    <smart-layout variant="staircase" horizontalAlign="center">
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Copilots</h4>
        <p>~$0.5T US software spend</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Task Agents</h4>
        <p>~$1.2T (+$0.7T services automation)</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Workflow Agents</h4>
        <p>~$2.8T (+$1.6T operational payroll)</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Autonomous</h4>
        <p>~$6.1T+ (+$3.2T knowledge payroll)</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
    </smart-layout>
  </div>
  <div>
    <img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/3jnHCsDFwRjsJDrh7fitP.png" meta="null" resize="null" source="image.custom">
    <aside variant="info"><b>Coding was the first domino.</b> GitHub code YoY growth hit ~35% in 2025. New iOS apps up ~45% YoY. New websites up ~32% YoY. AI is expanding software creation, not contracting it.</aside>
    <blockquote><p><i>"Every engineer is effectively gonna end up being more of like a tech lead in the future that has their own little army of engineering agents."</i><br>— Mark Zuckerberg</p></blockquote>
  </div>
</columns></div></section>

<section n="5" image-layout="blank" id="91aio264wv4xsm1">



    
    
  

<div class="body">
<h1>Software Stocks Are Down 20% YTD — But the Selloff Is Highly Uneven</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/wWckI890ODA5f8IbTfJWF.png" meta="null" resize="null" source="image.custom" align="center">
<smart-layout variant="solidBoxes" cellsize="10">
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Sector Performance YTD 2026</h4>
        <p>Energy: <b>+32%</b> | S&amp;P 500: <b>-5%</b> | Software: <b>-20%</b><br>Software is the worst-performing S&amp;P 500 sector in 2026.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Valuation Collapse</h4>
        <p>Public SaaS median NTM revenue multiple has collapsed to <b>4.1x</b> — near all-time lows, down from a peak of 22x in late 2020. The market is repricing the entire category.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>The Split Is Structural</h4>
        <p>Horizontal SaaS: <b>-35% LTM</b><br>Vertical SaaS: <b>+3%</b><br>Infrastructure: <b>+2%</b><br>The market is making a deliberate, informed distinction.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
    </smart-layout>
<aside variant="warning">Lowest-multiple names carry the highest daily volatility (R² = 0.50). Profitable growth commands a clear premium: <b>&gt;20% NTM growth companies trade at 8.1x</b> vs. only <b>2.9x for &lt;10% growers</b>.</aside>
<graph chart-type="bar" show-data-labels="true">
  <properties>
    <property key="category" label="Category" type="label">
    <property key="performance" label="LTM Performance (%)" type="data">
  </property></property></properties>
  <data>
    <row><cell>Energy</cell><cell>32</cell></row>
    <row><cell>S&amp;P 500</cell><cell>-5</cell></row>
    <row><cell>Infrastructure SaaS</cell><cell>2</cell></row>
    <row><cell>Vertical SaaS</cell><cell>3</cell></row>
    <row><cell>Horizontal SaaS</cell><cell>-35</cell></row>
  </data>
</graph></div></section>

<section n="6" image-layout="blank" id="lwyhfvat2gxzkiv">



    
  

<div class="body">
<h1>Why Horizontal SaaS Is Getting Hit Hardest</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/0ZENNVBzxXnKjBKg3SmMy.png" meta="null" resize="null" source="image.custom" align="center">
<smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine">
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4><span text-color="#2E7D32">Vertical SaaS (+3%) — Moats Are Real</span></h4>
        <p>Owns irreplaceable competitive advantages: proprietary industry data, compliance workflows, and years of embedded process history. AI can augment but cannot displace without re-acquiring that data. Switching cost is <b>existential, not cosmetic</b>.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4><span text-color="#1565C0">Infrastructure (+2%) — AI Is a Direct Tailwind</span></h4>
        <p>New AI workloads run on Snowflake, Datadog, MongoDB, Cloudflare. More AI deployment = more compute, data pipeline, and observability spend. Every incremental dollar of AI CapEx is infrastructure revenue.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4><span text-color="#B71C1C">Horizontal SaaS (-35%) — Accidentally Optimized for Replaceability</span></h4>
        <p>Designed to serve every industry equally, integrated deeply with none. Without proprietary data or long process history, switching is easier. These products track who does what and when — <b>exactly what AI handles well</b>. The product category is at structural risk.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
    </smart-layout>
<aside variant="caution"><b>The real concern is terminal value, not near-term earnings.</b> 85–95% of SaaS enterprise value comes from terminal value. A strong quarter moves &lt;5% of value. Investors are pricing in near-zero long-term growth — implied terminal growth rate dropped from <b>4.7% to 1.1%</b> in a single quarter for a representative $100B company (now $73B market cap). Multiples compress when the market loses faith in the terminal, not the next quarter.</aside></div></section>

