<section n="8" image-layout="blank" id="6owdj4f4xau1j0c">



    
  


<div class="body">
<h1>AI-Native Companies Are Growing at an Unprecedented Pace</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/xFqfSG07ZT7NxE8Z4aLa-.png" meta="null" resize="null" source="image.custom" align="center">
<smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithSideLine">
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Revenue Scale Is Real</h4>
        <p>OpenAI: <b>$25B current revenue run-rate</b>, projecting massive growth through 2028E. Anthropic: <b>$19B current run-rate</b>. These are not venture-subsidized metrics — they reflect genuine market demand at scale.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Structural Efficiency Advantage</h4>
        <p>AI-native companies achieve dramatically higher <b>ARR/FTE ratios</b> than traditional SaaS — a structural efficiency advantage that compounds over time and permanently changes the competitive economics of software.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
      <smart-layout-item>
        <h4>Capital Is Concentrating</h4>
        <p>VC funding is now <b>98% AI-native by Q1 2026</b> (vs. ~35% in Q1 2020). Top 20 deals captured <b>44% of all enterprise software VC funding in 2025</b>, up from just 8% in 2020. Model Labs (OpenAI, Anthropic, xAI) = 3% of deal count but <b>67% of dollars and 66% of enterprise value</b>.</p>
      </smart-layout-item>
    </smart-layout>
<graph chart-type="column" show-data-labels="true">
  <properties>
    <property key="year" label="Year" type="label">
    <property key="ai_share" label="Top 20 Deals Share of Enterprise SW VC (%)" type="data">
  </property></property></properties>
  <data>
    <row><cell>2020</cell><cell>8</cell></row>
    <row><cell>2021</cell><cell>6</cell></row>
    <row><cell>2022</cell><cell>7</cell></row>
    <row><cell>2023</cell><cell>23</cell></row>
    <row><cell>2024</cell><cell>31</cell></row>
    <row><cell>2025</cell><cell>44</cell></row>
  </data>
</graph>
<p>Capital concentration is accelerating dramatically. The top 20 deals now absorb nearly half of all enterprise software VC — a 5.5x increase in share since 2020.</p></div></section>

<section n="9" image-layout="blank" id="pq9kjwer0x7ktak">



    
    
    
  

<div class="body">
<h1>Private Market Valuations Are Surging — Rounds Are Closing Faster</h1><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/acme/uploaded-images/252NhEbSP3Be8Tp8zxBRu.png" meta="null" resize="null" source="image.custom" align="center">
<h4>Median Post-Money Valuations by Stage</h4>
<graph chart-type="column" show-data-labels="true">
      <properties>
        <property key="stage" label="Stage" type="label">
        <property key="q1_2024" label="Q1 2024 ($M)" type="data">
        <property key="q1_2026" label="Q1 2026 ($M)" type="data">
      </property></property></property></properties>
      <data>
        <row><cell>Seed</cell><cell>20</cell><cell>60</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Series A</cell><cell>80</cell><cell>150</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Series B</cell><cell>250</cell><cell>480</cell></row>
        <row><cell>Series C</cell><cell>600</cell><cell>1000</cell></row>
      </data>
    </graph>
<p>Seed median tripled from ~$20M to ~$60M in 24 months. Series C reached ~$1B. Valuation inflation is not uniform — it is concentrated in AI-native companies at every stage.</p>
<smart-layout variant="solidBoxes">
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>Private = New Public</h4>
    <p><b>24 private deals &gt;$1B</b> in 2025 vs. only <b>7 IPOs</b>. <b>86%</b> of US companies with $100M+ revenue are now private. The liquidity and price discovery function has migrated away from public markets.</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>Speed Is Accelerating</h4>
    <p>Median days between fundraising rounds has fallen sharply since mid-2023, reaching multi-year lows by early 2026. The best companies are not waiting — and the best terms go to the fastest movers.</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>The Historical Sweet Spot</h4>
    <p>Companies founded <b>4–6 years after a major platform shift</b> (Internet, Mobile, Cloud) have historically been the biggest value creators. For AI, that window is <b>2026–2027</b>. We are in it now.</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
</smart-layout></div></section>

<section n="10" image-layout="blank" id="u36zz5r4umr2idf">


<div class="body">
<h1>What This Means for Your Company Right Now</h1>
<smart-layout variant="outlineBoxesWithLabel" numbered="true" orientation="vertical">
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>Prove Your Moat Is Real</h4>
    <p>Investors and CIOs are asking the same question simultaneously: does your product own irreplaceable data, workflow, or compliance depth? If yes, quantify and communicate it explicitly. If not, the market will price that risk into your valuation before you're ready for it. Articulate switching cost in economic terms, not product terms.</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>Rethink Pricing Before Your Customers Do</h4>
    <p>Usage/outcome-based models are winning. <b>35% of CIOs</b> expect flat subscriptions to decline; <b>46%</b> expect usage/outcome models to expand. Proactively redesigning your pricing model is a retention strategy, not just a revenue optimization. Waiting until renewal creates unnecessary risk.</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>AI Is a Feature AND a Threat — Navigate Both</h4>
    <p><b>58% of CIOs</b> cite AI feature additions as the top driver of spend increases. But <b>45% of AI budgets</b> are replacing existing software. You must be on the right side of both trends simultaneously — AI-enabled enough to justify the spend, and differentiated enough not to be replaced by it.</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>Fundraising Windows Are Compressing</h4>
    <p>Rounds are closing faster, valuations are up sharply at every stage, but capital is concentrating in fewer deals. Top 20 deals captured <b>44% of enterprise software VC in 2025</b>. If you're raising, move with urgency. The best terms go to the fastest movers — and the market does not wait for slow-moving processes.</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
  <smart-layout-item>
    <h4>2026–2027 Is the Historical Sweet Spot</h4>
    <p>Every major platform shift — Internet, Mobile, Cloud — produced its biggest value creators from companies founded 4–6 years in. That is where we stand today for AI. The infrastructure is proven, adoption is at scale, and the application layer is not yet winner-take-all. The window is open. Use it.</p>
  </smart-layout-item>
</smart-layout></div></section>

